<section>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Two particles of masses 2 kg and 3 kg rest on a smooth horizontal
    surface, joined by a light inextensible string. A horizontal force of
    10 N is applied to the 3 kg particle, away from the 2 kg particle.
    Find the acceleration of the pair and the tension in the string.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>The surface is smooth, so the pair moves with the 10 N force.</li>
    <li>For the system: 10 = 5<i>a</i>, so <i>a</i> = 2 m/s²</li>
    <li>For the trailing 2 kg particle: <i>T</i> = 2 × 2 = 4 N</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The surface is now rough, with coefficient of friction 0.2. The masses
    and the force are unchanged. Find <i>a</i> and <i>T</i>.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Total friction <i>F</i> = 0.2 × 5 × 9.81 = 9.81 N</li>
    <li>10 N &gt; 9.81 N, so the pair still moves.</li>
    <li>For the system: 10 − 9.81 = 5<i>a</i>, so <i>a</i> = 0.038 m/s²</li>
    <li><i>T</i> = 2 × 10 / 5 = 4 N, unchanged, because both particles share the same <i>μ</i></li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Friction reduces the acceleration but not the tension. If
    <i>P</i> were no greater than <i>μ</i>(<i>m</i>₁ + <i>m</i>₂)<i>g</i>,
    the pair would remain at rest.
  </p>
</section>
